  "INTRODUCTION\nDuring the period spanning from around 1000 to 1200 CE, \nNorthern India witnessed a tumultuous era characterized as \nthe age of conflict. This epoch marked the disintegration of \nthe tripartite powers, namely the Rashtrakutas, the Palas, \nand the Pratiharas, giving rise to numerous smaller kingdoms \nacross the region. \nThe demise of the Pratihara empire, for instance, led to the \nemergence of various Rajput states governed by distinct \nRajput dynasties, including the Chahamanas (Chauhans), \nChandellas, and the Paramaras of Malwa. Despite their \nresistance to Turkish incursions from northwest India, \nspearheaded by Mahmud of Ghazni and Mohammad Ghori \nin the 11th and 12th centuries, these states, fragmented and \nlacking unity, succumbed to defeat against the invaders.\nThe disarray among the Rajput states, stemming from their \ninability to unite against external threats, ultimately paved \nthe way for their downfall in the face of Turkish assaults.\nTHE RAJPUT CLANS\nIn the early 12th century, the term Rajput gained prominence, \nsignifying specific lineage-based clans within Rajput society. \nThe foundational element of Rajput’s social structure was \nthe clan, with each tracing its lineage to a common ancestor, \nwhether real or mythical. \nOrigin\n The origins of the Rajputs have been a subject of \nhistorical debate, with some scholars suggesting descent \nfrom foreign groups like the Shakas, Kushanas, and \nHunas who settled in Rajasthan and integrated into \nIndian society, evolving into a warrior class. \n Alternatively, others view them as an integral part of the \nKshatriya varna in the Brahmanical system. \nEmergence and Structure of Rajput Clans\n Scholar B.D. Chattopadhyaya proposes that Rajput clans \nemerged due to various factors, including the expansion \nof the agrarian economy, land distribution among royal \nkinsmen, and the subsequent rise of local chiefs to \nindependent authority. \n Inter-clan collaboration through political and matrimonial \nalliances, coupled with extensive fortress construction, \ncontributed to the consolidation of the clan structure. \nThese clans exerted dominance over specific territories, \nemphasizing attachment to land, family, and ‘honour’ as \ndefining characteristics.\nNorthern India \n(c. 1000- 1200 CE) 3\nFig. 3.1: Kingdoms in the period of 900-1200 AD"
