  "Northern India (c. 1000- 1200 CE) 31\nPrithviraj III (c.1178−1192 CE)\nFig. 3.4: Prithviraj Chauhan\n A prodigious military leader, Prithviraj Chauhan secured \nvictories against a formidable array of adversaries, \nincluding Chandella king Paramardi, Chalukya Bhima II, \nand Gahadvala Jayachandra.\n Ascending to the throne at the tender age of 11, following \nthe demise of his father Someshvara, Prithviraj Chauhan \nassumed the reins of administration five years later, at \nthe age of 16.\n Prithviraj Chauhan’s military prowess shone through in \nhis expedition against the Chandella ruler of Bundelkhand \nand their capital, Mahoba. This pivotal conflict witnessed \nthe tragic loss of the renowned Chandella warriors Alha \nand Udal.\n First Battle of Tarain: Prithviraj Chauhan emerged \nvictorious against Muhammad Ghori in the First Battle of \nTarain, fought around 1191 CE.\n In 1192 CE, Muhammad Ghori, seeking retribution, \ndefeated Prithviraj Chauhan in the Second Battle of Tarain. \nThe vanquished ruler was subsequently executed, marking \na pivotal turning point in the Islamic conquest of India.\n Prithviraj Chauhan’s defeat at Tarain has been immortalized \nin various semi-legendary accounts, with Prithviraj Raso\nstanding as the most notable literary tribute.\n Two epic poems, the Prithviraj Raso and the Prithviraj \nVijaya, were composed by Prithviraj Chauhan’s court \npoets, Chandbardai and Jayanaka, respectively, \nperpetuating his legacy.\nHari-Raja (c. 1193−1194 CE)\n Following his victory at the Second Battle of Tarain, \nMuhammad Ghori extended his dominion by capturing \nthe cities of Ajmer and Delhi.\n Prithviraj Chauhan’s young son, Govinda, was installed \nas a vassal in Ajmer by Muhammad Ghori.\n Govinda’s submission to Muslim authority resulted in his \ndethronement and banishment by Prithviraj Chauhan’s \nbrother, Hari-Raja.\n Undeterred, Govinda established the Chahamana branch of \nRanastambhapura (Ranthambore) to continue his lineage.\n Hari-raja, determined to reclaim the Chauhan dynasty’s \nlost glory, reconquered Ajmer and adopted a hostile \nstance against Delhi’s newly established Muslim rulers.\n Qutabuddin Aibak, Muhammad Ghori’s successor, \nlaunched an invasion of Ajmer, overthrew Hari-Raja, and \nannexed the principality under his control.\n The Chauhan dynasty, weakened by these successive \ndefeats, retreated to Ranthambore, their power gradually \nfading.\n In c.1301 CE, Ala-ud-din Khilji, the Sultan of Delhi, led \na formidable assault on Ranthambore, successfully \ncapturing the fortress and effectively dismantling the last \nstronghold of Chauhan power.\nTHE CHANDELLAS \nOF BUNDELKHAND\nOrigin\n The Chandellas of Bundelkhand trace their lineage to the \nmythical ancestor Chandratreya, believed to be born of \nthe moon. \n Initially serving as feudatories of the Gurjara−Pratiharas \nof Kannauj, the Chandellas engaged in conflicts with \nneighboring powers such as the Pratiharas, the Palas, \nand the Kalachuris of Chedi, situated to the south of the \nChandella domain. \nArtistic and Architectural Legacy\n Renowned for their artistic and architectural \ncontributions, the Chandellas left an indelible mark with \nthe construction of Nagara-style temples in their original \ncapital, Khajuraho. \n Notable examples include the Lakshmana Temple (c. \n930–950 CE), Vishvanatha Temple (c. 999-1002 CE), and \nKandariya Mahadeva Temple (c. 1030 CE), erected during \nthe reigns of Chandella rulers Yashovarman, Dhanga, and \nVidyadhara, respectively.\nKhajuraho Group of Monuments (UNESCO World \nHeritage Site-Cultural)\n The Khajuraho temples, constructed during the peak \nof the Chandella dynasty between 950 CE and 1050 \nCE, now number around 20. \n These temples, categorised into three groups, represent \ntwo distinct religions – Hinduism and Jainism.\n They achieve a harmonious blend of architectural \nand sculptural excellence. One notable example is \nthe Kandariya Temple, adorned with an abundance of \nsculptures, ranking among the finest masterpieces in \nIndian art."
