  "32 Medieval India \nPatronage and Expansion\n The Chandellas demonstrated their patronage of the arts \nbeyond Khajuraho, commissioning various water bodies, \npalaces, and forts in locations such as Jayapura-Durga \n(modern Ajaigarh), Kalanjara (modern Kalinjar), and their \nlater capital, Mahotsava-Nagara (modern Mahoba). \n Nannuka (831 CE-845 CE), the founder of the dynasty, \nestablished the Chandella rule in the early 9th century \nCE, laying the foundation for a dynasty that would \nsignificantly contribute to the cultural and architectural \nheritage of the region.\nThe Chandella Kings\nNannuka\n Reigned around 831-845 CE, is recognized as the founding \nking of the Chandella dynasty.\nVakpati\n Vakpati (845-865 CE) is credited in Chandella inscriptions \nwith defeating numerous adversaries. \nJayashakti (Jeja) and Vijayashakti (Vija)\n Following Vakpati, his sons Jayashakti (Jeja) and \nVijayashakti (Vija) (865-885 CE) played pivotal roles in \nconsolidating Chandella’s power. \n An inscription in Mahoba reveals that the Chandella \nterritory was named “Jejakabhukti” after Jayashakti.\nRahila\n Rahila (885-905 CE), the successor to Vijayashakti, earned \nacclaim in eulogistic inscriptions for his military triumphs.\nShri Harshadev (905-925 CE)\n Shri Harshadev, Rahila’s son, played a significant role around \n914 CE in restoring the rule of Pratihara King Mahipala, after \na Rashtrakuta invasion led by King Indra III.\nYashovarman\n Yashovarman, reigning from around 925 to 950 CE, \nachieved practical independence in the 10th century \nwhile still acknowledging Pratihara’s suzerainty.\n Yashovarman conquered the fortress of Kalanjara and \nachieved several military successes against various \nadversaries, including the Gaudas (identified with the Palas), \nKhasas, Chedis (Kalachuris of Tripuri), Kosalas (possibly \nSomavamshis), Mithila (a potential tributary ruler), Malavas \n(Paramaras), Kurus, Kashmiris, and Gurjaras.\n His reign marked the initiation of the renowned \nChandella style of art and architecture, highlighted by the \ncommissioning of the Lakshmana Temple in Khajuraho\naround 954 CE.\nFig. 3.5: Lakshmana Temple at Khajuraho\nDhanga-Deva\n Reigning from around 950 to 999 CE, he was the first \nindependent Chandella king to adopt the title of \nMaharajadhiraja, signifying the sovereignty of the \nChandella dynasty.\n Under Dhanga’s rule, the Chandellas became a sovereign \npower, and their records no longer mentioned Pratihara’s \noverlordship.\n A Khajuraho inscription claimed that rulers of Kosala, \nKratha, Kuntala, and Simhala humbly obeyed Dhanga’s \ncommands, showcasing his extensive influence.\n Dhanga’s military campaigns were highlighted in \ninscriptions, with claims that the wives of kings from \nAndhra, Anga, Kanchi, and Radha resided in his prisons \nas a result of his victorious wars.\n Dhanga, like his predecessor Yashovarman, \ncommissioned a magnificent Vishvanatha Temple in \nKhajuraho, contributing to the legacy of Chandella art \nand architecture.\nFig. 3.6: Vishvanatha Temple at Khajuraho\nGanda-Deva\n Ganda-Deva, who ruled from around 999 to 1002 \nCE, seems to have maintained the territorial integrity \ninherited from his predecessor, Dhanga.\nVidyadhara\n Vidyadhara, reigning from approximately 1003 to 1035 \nCE, is associated with a legend that suggests he killed the \nPratihara king of Kannauj (possibly Rajyapala). This act \nwas in response to the Pratihara king fleeing his capital \ninstead of facing the Ghaznavid invader, Mahmud of \nGhazni.\n Subsequently, Mahmud invaded Vidyadhara’s kingdom, \nleading to a resolution where Vidyadhara paid tribute to \nMahmud. By the end of Vidyadhara’s rule, the Ghaznavid \ninvasions had significantly weakened the Chandella \nkingdom."
