  "Northern India (c. 1000- 1200 CE) 33\n Exploiting the weakened state of the Chandella kingdom, \nthe Kalachuri king Gangeya-deva conquered the Eastern \nparts of the territory.\n Vidyadhara’s notable contribution was the commissioning \nof the Kandariya Mahadeva Temple, adding to the \narchitectural and artistic legacy of the Chandellas.\nFig. 3.7: Kandariya Mahadeva Temple, Khajuraho\nVijayapala\n Vijayapala, reigning from around 1035 to 1050 CE, \nachieved a victory over Gangeya-deva in a battle. \nHowever, Chandella’s power began to decline during his \nreign. The Kachchhapaghatas of Gwalior likely ended \ntheir allegiance to the Chandellas during this period.\n The subsequent rulers faced a gradual decline in power \nas they engaged in numerous battles with neighboring \ndynasties, particularly the Paramaras of Malwa and the \nKalachuris of Tripuri. \n Decline: Starting in the 11th century, the Chandellas \nencountered raids from Northern Muslim dynasties, \nincluding the Ghaznavids and the Ghurids. The effective \nend of Chandella’s power occurred around the beginning \nof the 13th century due to invasions by the Chahamanas \nand Ghurids.\nParamardi Deva\n Paramardi-deva, reigning from around 1165 to 1203 CE, \nemerged as the last powerful Chandella king. In 1183 CE, \nPrithviraj Chauhan of the Chahamana dynasty invaded \nthe Chandella kingdom, sacking Mahoba, according to \nthe Madanpur stone inscriptions. Paramardi, possibly in \na fit of cowardice, sought refuge in the Kalanjara fort.\n There are different accounts of Paramardi’s fate. \nAccording to some legends, he either committed suicide \nout of shame or retired to Gaya. Other versions suggest \nthat Paramardi restored Chandella’s power and ruled \nindependently until around 1203 CE when the Delhi \nSultanate invaded the Chandella kingdom.\n Contrarily, historians such as the 16th-century scholar \nFerishta propose that Paramardi was assassinated by his \nown minister, who disagreed with the king’s decision to \nsurrender to the Delhi forces.\nVeerangana Rani Durgavati \n In October 2023, 500th birth anniversary of \nVeerangana Rani Durgavati was celebrated. She was \nborn in Chandel Dynasty of Mahoba in Banda, UP \nand was married to Dalpatshah of Gond Dynasty of \nGarhaKatanga in 1542. \n She took the reins of the Gond Dynasty in 1550 after \nDalpatshah’s death as the guardian of her son Veer \nNarayan. \n She was a contemporary of the Mughal emperor \nAkbar. During her reign, Akbar attacked the Gond \nEmpire under the leadership of Asaf Khan. She \nopposed Mughal expansionism.\nContributions \n She constructed reservoirs such as Ranital, Cherital \nand Adhartal and moved the Capital to Chauragarh \n(from Singaurgarh) on the strategic Satpura Hill Range. \n She allowed Acharya Bitthalnath to establish a seat \nof the Pushtimarg Cult at Garha. She welcomed the \nVitthalnath of Vallabh community and took Diksha \nfrom him.\n Victory over Baz Bahadur of Malwa: Tarikh-i-Firishta \nmentions that Durgavati defeated Baz Bahadur, the \nruler of Malwa. \n Story of Durgavati was chronicled by Abul Fazl (Akbar’s \nchronicler) and British Colonel, Sleeman.\nFollowing their defeat against the Delhi Sultanate, the \nChandella’s power failed to fully recover. The continued \ndecline of the Chandella authority was attributed to the \nincreasing influence of Islam and the emergence of local \ndynasties like the Bundelas, the Baghelas, and the Khangars. \nA minor branch of the Chandella family persisted in ruling \nKalanjara, but their ruler met his demise at the hands of \nSher Shah Suri’s army in 1545 CE. Another minor branch \nmaintained rule in Mahoba, and certain texts indicate that \none of its princesses entered into matrimony with the Gond \nroyal family of Mandla.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nDo you think the depiction of warfare and eroticism \nin Chandella temple sculptures primarily served \nreligious and symbolic purposes, or were they also \ninfluenced by social realities and political context of \nthe period?"
