    body: [
  "Northern India (c. 1000- 1200 CE) 35\n Demonstrating exceptional military prowess, Sindhuraja\nsecured victories against various formidable adversaries. \nHis conquests included triumphant campaigns against a \nHuna chief, the Somavanshis of south Kosala, the Shilaharas \nof Konkana, and the ruler of Lata in southern Gujarat.\n His biography, titled “Nava-Sahasanka-Charita,” was \npenned by his court poet Padmagupta.\nBhoja (c.1010−1055 CE)\n Bhoja, Sindhuraja’s son and Munja’s nephew, stands \nas the most celebrated ruler of the Paramara dynasty, \ntaking it to its zenith.\n Under Bhoja’s reign, the Paramaras expanded their \nkingdom from Chittor in the north to upper Konkan in \nthe south and from the Sabarmati River in the west to \nVidisha in the east.\n In approximately 1018 CE, Bhoja defeated the Chalukyas of \nLata in present-day Gujarat, and between 1018 CE and 1020 \nCE, he gained control of the Northern Konkan, potentially \nhaving the Shilahara rulers as his brief feudatories.\n Alliance: Bhoja formed an alliance against Kalyani \nChalukya King Jayasimha II, joined by Rajendra Chola and \nGangeya-deva Kalachuri.\n• In the later years of Bhoja’s reign, around 1042 \nCE, Jayasimha’s son Someshvara I invaded Malwa, \nsacking its capital, Dhara. Although Bhoja regained \ncontrol, the defeat shifted his southern boundary \nfrom Godavari to Narmada.\n Restricted by Kachchhapaghatas and Chandellas: Bhoja’s \nattempts to expand eastwards were thwarted by the \nChandella king Vidyadhara, but he extended influence \namong the Chandella feudatories, the Kachchhapaghatas \nof Dubkund.\n• Bhoja launched a campaign against the \nKachchhapaghatas of Gwalior, aiming for Kannauj, \nbut was repulsed by their ruler, Kirtiraja.\n Titles and Influence: Adopting the title of Parameshvara￾Paramabhattaraka, akin to Paramara-Deva, Bhoja gained \nrecognition.\n• Some Muslim historians suggest Mahmud of Ghazni \naltered his route after sacking Somnath to avoid a \nconfrontation with a Hindu king identified as Param \nDev, believed to be Bhoja.\n Bhoja possibly contributed troops to support Hindu \nShahi ruler Anandapala against the Ghaznavids and may \nhave been part of the Hindu alliance expelling Mahmud’s \ngovernors from Hansi, Thanesar, and surrounding areas \naround 1043 CE.\n In the last year of Bhoja’s reign, or shortly after his \ndeath, Solanki king Bhima-deva and Kalachuri king Karna \nattacked his kingdom. Some historians propose that \nBhoja died of a disease during the Allied army’s attack.\n Allround Contributions: Recognized as a righteous \nscholar, king, and polymath, Bhoja’s writings spanned \ngrammar, poetry, architecture, yoga, and chemistry. He \npromoted arts, literature, and sciences, establishing the \nBhoj Shala for Sanskrit studies and a Sarasvati Temple in \npresent-day Dhar.\n• Bhoja is credited with founding the city of Bhojpur \nand overseeing the construction of the Bhojeshwar \nTemple and three dams in the area. He is often \nlikened to the legendary Vikramaditya.\nJayasimha I (c.1055−1070 CE)\n Jayasimha I, Bhoja’s son and successor, confronted a \njoint invasion by the Kalachuris and Solankis shortly after \nBhoja’s demise. \n Bilhana’s writings imply that he sought assistance from \nthe Chalukyas of Kalyani.\nLakshmadeva (c.1086−1094 CE)\n Lakshmadeva is credited with extensive military \nconquests, as indicated by the Nagpur Prashasti\ninscription dated around 1105 CE.\nIn 1305 CE, Mahalakadeva, the last identified Paramara \nking, suffered defeat and was killed by the forces led by Ala￾ud-din Khilji of Delhi. However, epigraphic evidence from \nan inscription in Udaipur implies that the Paramara rule \npersisted for a few years following Mahalakadeva’s demise, \nextending until around 1310 CE, particularly in the north￾Eastern region of Malwa. Subsequent inscriptions reveal \nthat, by around 1338 CE, the Delhi Sultanate had successfully \ncaptured the area.\nTHE CHALUKYAS OF GUJARAT/\nTHE SOLANKI RAJPUTS\nSolanki Rajputs\n The Solanki Rajputs, also recognized as the Chalukya \nfamily of Gujarat, distinguished themselves from the \nearlier Chalukyan dynasty of Badami. \n Their reign extended across parts of Gujarat and \nKathiawar from approximately 950 to 1300 CE. \n Unlike the Chalukyas of Kalyani and Vengi, the Solankis \ndid not assert a shared lineage or any connection to the \noriginal Chalukya dynasty. \n• Intriguingly, they opted for the variant ‘Chaulukya’\nrather than using the term ‘Chalukya’ to self-identify.\nProminent Branches of the Solanki Dynasty\n The Solanki dynasty comprised several branches, with \nthree standing out prominently. \n• Mattamayura Branch: The oldest branch, led by \nrulers Simhavarman, Sadhanva, and Avanivarman, \nheld sway over Central India’s Mattamayura. \n• Bhrigukachchha Branch: Another branch, founded \nby Barappa in Lata (South Gujarat), established the \nnotable city of Bhrigukachchha (Broach) as its center. \n• Anahilapataka Branch: The third branch, initiated \nby Mularaja I, chose Anahilapataka (modern Patan, \nGujarat) as its capital."
]
