  "36 Medieval India \nTHE SOLANKI KINGS\nMularaja I (c. 940–995 CE)\n Mularaja I conducted numerous military expeditions \nagainst Saurashtra, Kutch, and the Abhiras, among \nothers.\n His power faced setbacks due to invasions by the \nChahamanas and the Chalukyas of Lata.\n After a defeat by the Paramaras of Malwa, Mularaja \nI sought refuge with the Rashtrakuta king, Dhavala. \nEventually, he reclaimed his kingdom, but his successors \ncontinued to engage in conflicts with the Kalachuris and \nParamaras.\n Despite being a Shaiva devotee, Mularaja I supported \nJainism, as evidenced by the construction of the \nMulavasatika temple for the Digambaras and the \nMulanatha-jinadeva temple for the Shvetambaras.\nBhima I (c. 1022 – 1064 CE)\n Bhima I, the grandson of Mularaja, emerged as a \nsignificant ruler following Mularaja’s reign.\n Somnath Plunder: During his rule, Gujarat fell under the \nonslaught of Mahmud of Ghazni, who plundered Somnath \nin 1025 CE.\n Cultural Contributions:\n• Bhima I was responsible for the construction of the \nSun Temple of Modhera.\n• In his memory, his wife Udaymati commissioned the \nconstruction of Rani-ki Vav (Queen’s Stepwell), which \nhas been designated as a UNESCO World Heritage \nSite.\nFig. 3.8: Rani Ki Vav\n• Notably, one of the five renowned Dilwara temples, \nthe Vimal Vasahi temple dedicated to the Jain lord \nRishabha, was built around 1031 CE by Vimal Shah, a \nminister of Bhima I.\nFig. 3.9: Vimal Vasahi Temple, Mount Abu\nKaran (c. 1064-1092 CE)\n Karan founded Karnavati, now known as Ahmedabad, \nafter defeating a Bhil chieftain.\nJayasimha Siddharaja (c. 1092 – 1142 CE)\n Jayasimha Siddharaja ruled for approximately half a \ncentury, expanding the kingdom into an empire.\n Jayasimha’s conquests extended beyond Saurashtra \nand Kutch, encompassing the acquisition of Malwa. \nA popular legend among Gujarat bards revolves \naround the siege of Junagadh by Jayasimha.\n Despite the fort’s capture, the wife of the ruler of \nJunagadh, Ranakdevi chose to commit sati rather than \nremarry Jayasimha. He agreed to let her perform sati on \na pyre at Wadhwan. \n• The Ranakdevi Temple now stands at the site of her \nsacrifice.\n Hemachandra, a Jain monk, gained prominence during \nJayasimha’s reign, fostering a good relationship with the \nking.\n The Rudra Mahakala Temple at Siddhapur was \nconstructed during Jayasimha Siddharaja’s rule.\nKumarapala (c. 1142−1171 CE)\n Under Kumarapala’s reign, Gujarat reached the pinnacle \nof prosperity. \n His rule was characterized by flourishing economic and \ncultural landscapes. \n Kumarapala fostered a positive relationship with \nHemachandra, actively endorsing Jainism and overseeing \nthe reconstruction of the Somnath temple.\nBhima II (c. 1177−1240 CE)\n Bhima II emerged as a defender against the incursions \nof Muhammad Ghori. Notably, at the Battle of Kayadara, \nBhima II successfully repelled Ghori’s ambitions to \nreplicate the feats of Mahmud of Ghazni. \n In a subsequent invasion led by Ghori’s deputy Aibak \nin c. 1192 CE, Bhima II once again emerged victorious, \nearning the title of Abhinav Siddharaj in recognition of \nhis valor and resilience.\nAround 1243 CE, the Solankis relinquished control of Gujarat \nto their feudatories, the Hindu Vaghela dynasty of Dholka, \ninitiating a brief yet influential principality lasting for 76 \nyears. By approximately 1292 CE, the Vaghelas became \nsubjects of the Seuna (Yadava) dynasty based in Devagiri \non the Deccan Plateau. The Vaghela dynasty’s conclusion \nunfolded with the conquest of Gujarat by Khilji around 1299 \nCE. In the same period, two Porwad brothers, Vastupal and \nTejpal, who served as ministers to Virdhaval, the Vaghela \nruler of Gujarat, erected the Luna Vashi temple dedicated to \nLord Neminath in c. 1230 CE."
