    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: [
  "Northern India (c. 1000- 1200 CE) 37\nTHE TOMARAS\nThe Tomaras governed the region of Hariyana (Haryana) \nwith their capital at Dhillika, known as Delhi. Considered \nfeudatories of the Pratiharas, medieval bardic literature \nreferred to them as the “Tuar” dynasty, classifying them \namong the 36 Rajput clans. Their history is marked by \nconflicts with the Chahamanas of Shakambhari, ultimately \nsucceeded by the Chahamanas. A 13th-century Palam Baoli \ninscription (found in a step well in Palam village) traces \nthe lineage of Hariyanaka’s rulers from the Tomaras to the \nChauhans and, finally, the Delhi Sultans.\nAnangapala Tomara\n The renowned iron pillar at Mehrauli carries an 11th￾century inscription honoring Anangapala Tomara, a \nnotable ruler credited with establishing Delhi. \n His coins, featuring the horseman-and-bull figure \nand the title “Shri Samanta-deva,” resemble those of \nthe Shakambhari Chahamana kings Someshvara and \nPrithviraja III, indicating his contemporary status with \nthese 12th-century monarchs.\nLegend of Dhilli (Delhi) Origin\n A legend recounted in the Prithviraja Raso links the \nTomaras to the foundation of Delhi. \n According to the tale, a Brahmana foretold Anangapala \nTomar that the iron pillar’s base rested on the hood of \nVasuki, the serpent king, and his rule would endure as \nlong as the pillar stood upright. \n• Anangapala, curious, excavated the pillar, discovering \nit smeared with Vasuki’s blood. \n• Realizing his error, he ordered its reinstatement, but \nit remained loose, leading to the area being named \n“Dhilli” (modern Delhi).\nContributions to Delhi’s Waterworks\n The Tomaras left a lasting impact on Delhi’s infrastructure, \ncredited with the construction of early waterworks. \n Anangapala II, the founder of the Lal Kot citadel in \nMehrauli, initiated the creation of the Anang Tal tank. \n• Additionally, the renowned Suraj Kund reservoir near \nFaridabad is attributed to Surajpala, another Tomara \nking.\nFig. 3.10: Surajkund Reservoir\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nDid the Tomaras, seen as Pratihara vassals, exert \nindependent influence in shaping the history of Delhi \nand Haryana, and has their legacy been overshadowed \nby later dynasties, considering their significant \ncontributions and environmental priorities?\nTHE GAHADAVALAS OF KANNAUJ\nThe Gahadvalas, also known as Gaharwars, asserted their \nSuryavanshi Kshatriya lineage as they governed the kingdom \nof Kannauj for approximately a century, commencing in the \nlate 11th century. Notably, the Rathore clan of the Rajputs \nclaimed descent from the Gahadvala dynasty, establishing a \nsignificant link between these lineages.\nThe inception of the Gahadavala dynasty of Kannauj can be \nattributed to Chandradeva, who laid the foundation around \nc. 1090 CE. Over time, they systematically displaced the Palas \nfrom Bihar and elevated Banaras to the status of a secondary \ncapital in their expanding domain.\nChandradeva (around c. 1090 CE)\n Chandradeva, the founding ruler of the Gahadavala \ndynasty, achieved notable success by seizing control of \nDelhi from both the Pratiharas and the Rashtrakutas.\n He successfully integrated Delhi into his Kannauj state.\nGovind Chandra (c.1114−1154 CE)\n The grandson of Chandradeva, Govind Chandra, not \nonly emerged victorious against the Ghaznavids but also \nplayed a pivotal role in securing the release of his father, \nMadan Chandra. \n Remarkably, during his reign, Govindachandra elevated \nKannauj to unprecedented glory, propelling the state of \nGaharwar to the pinnacle of its power.\n Govindachandra’s kingdom extended across a \nsignificant expanse, encompassing much of present-day \nUttar Pradesh and Bihar.\n Govindachandra distinguished himself as a patron of both \nHindu temples and Buddhist monasteries, showcasing a \ncommitment to cultural diversity within his realm.\n Govindachandra’s wife, Kumaradevi, stood out as a devout \nBuddhist, highlighting the dynasty’s embrace of different \nreligious traditions during Govindachandra’s reign.\nVijayachandra (c.1154−1170 CE)\n Son of Govindachandra, also known as Vijayapala/\nMalladeva.\n Successfully defended against Ghaznavid’s aggressions.\n However, during his rule, Delhi was lost, and the Tomaras \nceased to recognize Vijayachandra as their sovereign, \npossibly aligning with the Chauhans of Ajmer."
]
