    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: [
  "38 Medieval India \nJaichandra (c. 1170 − 1194 CE)\n Son of Vijayachandra, the last prominent king of the \nGahadavala dynasty.\n The dynasty’s power and vast territory drew attention \neven from Muslim historians.\n During his reign, Delhi was annexed by the Chauhans \nfrom Ajmer, led by Prithviraj Chauhan.\n According to some legends, a romantic connection \nbetween Prithviraj and Jaichandra’s daughter, Samyukta, \nled to elopement and heightened animosity between \nthe kingdoms.\n Seeking help from Muslims in the north to counter the \nChauhans, Jaichandra’s actions became the subject \nof folklore. He faced defeat at the hands of King \nLakshmansena of Bengal.\n Fall of Kannauj: After the fall of Delhi around c.1194 CE, \nKannauj was attacked by Muhammad of Ghori, resulting \nin Jaichandra’s defeat in the Battle of Chanawar and his \npurported drowning in the Ganga.\n• Jaichandra’s kingdom was conquered and sacked by \nGhori’s armies.\nAfter the fall of Kannauj and the Gahadavala dynasty due to \nIltutmish’s conquest, some survivors, notably Jaichandra’s \nson and grandson Siyaji (Shiv ji), sought refuge in the Marwar \ndesert region of Rajasthan. In the early 13th century, they laid \nthe foundation for the Rathore clan, a lineage that would go on \nto rule the princely state of Marwar, known as Jodhpur.\nThe glory of Kannauj came to a definitive close with the \nconquest by Iltutmish, signalling the end of an era for the \nGahadavala dynasty and paving the way for the Rathores to \ncarve their destiny in the sands of Marwar.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nKnowing that Gahadavalas’ primary legacy rests \non their political and military achievements, such \nas resisting the Ghaznavids and expanding their \nterritory, enumerate their contributions in domains \nof religion and culture.\nKINGDOM OF KASHMIR \nAND NORTH-WEST\nThe historical accounts of Kashmir and Northwest India find \ntheir roots in the well-known chronicle titled Rajatarangini, \nwhich translates to ‘the river of Kings.’ Composed in Sanskrit, \nthis significant work is believed to have been written in the \n12th century CE by a Kashmiri Brahmana named Kalhana. \nDespite the limited information available about Kalhana, it \nis speculated that his father, Champaka, served as a minister \nin the court of Harsha of Kashmir. The epic comprises 7826 \nverses, organized into eight books referred to as Tarangas, \neach resembling a wave in its narrative flow.\nKARKOTA DYNASTY\nIn the early 7th century, the Karkota dynasty (c. 625–855 \nCE) emerged as the ruling authority in Kashmir, marking a \ntransformative period in the region’s ascendancy as a power \nin Central Asia and Northern India. The Karkota emperors, \nprimarily adherents of Hinduism, gained recognition for \nconstructing magnificent Hindu temples within their capital, \nParihaspur. Despite their Hindu focus, the dynasty also \nextended patronage to Buddhism, evident in the presence \nof stupas, chaityas, and viharas within the remnants of their \ncapital.\nDurlabh Vardhana (c. 625−661 CE)\n The founder of the Karkota dynasty established their \nrule during the lifetime of the Pushyabhuti king, \nHarshavardhana.\n Also recognized as Prajnaditya, Durlabh Vardhana’s reign \nwitnessed the visit of the renowned Chinese traveller \nHsuan Tsang to Kashmir.\nLalitaditya/Muktapida(c.697−733 CE)\n Regarded as the most illustrious king of the Karkota \ndynasty, Lalitaditya achieved remarkable feats by \nexpanding Kashmir’s influence beyond its traditional \nmountainous boundaries.\n Military Expeditions: He emerged victorious against the \nTurks, Tibetans, Bhutias, Kambojas, and others, although \nsome historians question the extent of these conquests \nas possibly exaggerated by Kalhana, the chronicler of the \ntime.\n• Lalitaditya’s significant triumph was over King \nYasovarman of Kannauj.\n Architectural Contribution: In addition to his military \nsuccesses, he commissioned the construction of the \nrenowned Martand (Sun) Temple in the Anantnag\ndistrict of Kashmir.\n• This temple, dedicated to the Sun God, stands as the \noldest known Sun temple in India and was among the \nlargest temple complexes of its era.\nFig. 3.11: Martand Sun Temple\nVajraditya (c.734−741 CE)\n During Vajraditya’s rule, Kashmir faced frequent raids by \nArabs."
]
