    id: "m3-pdf-page-40",
    chapter: "03 • Northern India (c. 1000–1200 CE)",
    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 40 — Northern India (c. 1000- 1200 CE) 39 Jayapida (c.745−776 CE)  Considered one of the more powerful Karkota kings,",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: [
  "Northern India (c. 1000- 1200 CE) 39\nJayapida (c.745−776 CE)\n Considered one of the more powerful Karkota kings, \nJayapida undertook numerous military expeditions.\n• Led a three-year-long campaign against the Eastern \ncountries, achieving victory over the five chieftains of \nGauda and the ruler of Kannauj.\n Towards the end of his reign, Jayapida is believed to have \nimposed excessive taxes on the advice of Kayasthas and \ncruelly treated his subjects.\n According to legends, he met his demise due to a curse \nby a Brahmana.\nUTPALA DYNASTY\nDuring the transformative period from the 8th to the 10th \ncenturies CE, the Hindu Utpala dynasty left an enduring \nimprint on the political landscape of Kashmir. Avanti Varman, \nthe dynasty’s founder, ascended to power in 855 CE, marking \nthe culmination of the Karkota dynasty’s rule.\nPolitical Dynamics of Early Medieval \nKashmir\n The political landscape of early medieval Kashmir \ninvolved the significant influence of the tantrins (a body \nof foot soldiers), ekangas (a body of royal bodyguards), \nand Damaras (landed chiefs).\n• These groups not only played crucial roles in the \npolitics of the time but also occasionally acted as \nkingmakers.\nLegacy of Powerful Queens\n The history of the Kashmir region stands out for its \nunique tradition of powerful queens.\n The Rajatarangini, a historical chronicle, highlights three \nnotable women rulers:\n• Yashovati, belonging to the Gonanda dynasty, is \nrecognized for her influential rule.\n• Sugandha, representing the Utpala dynasty, played a \nsignificant role as a woman ruler.\n• Didda, from the Yashaskara dynasty, had the longest \nand most eventful stint among the mentioned queens, \nearning her a detailed discussion in historical records.\nAvanti Varman (c. 855−883 CE)\n The founder of the Utpala dynasty, Avanti Varman, \nearned a reputation as a great administrator.\n Implemented measures to stabilize the state and regain \ncontrol over the Damaras, addressing internal issues \ncaused by rural aristocrats.\n Recognized as a benevolent and beloved king, he focused \non ensuring prosperity across the region.\n Notable contributions include innovative irrigation \nprojects, such as preventing flood waters in the \nMahapadma (Wular) lake from damaging crops.\n He founded the cities of Avantipur and Suyapur during \nhis reign.\n Constructed numerous Hindu temples dedicated to Vishnu \nand Shiva, along with Buddhist monasteries, including the \nrenowned Avantiswara and Avantiswami temples.\nSankara Varman (c. 883−902 CE)\n Son of Avanti Varman, Sankara Varman led military \ncampaigns in Punjab and Gujarat.\n His military exploits resulted in significant wealth \ndepletion from the Treasury, leading to the imposition of \nheavy taxes on the population.\n However, his attempts to recover losses through fiscal \nextortion, including plundering temples, were insufficient \nto replenish the treasury.\nYASHASKARA DYNASTY \nOF KASHMIR\nDidda (c. 958 – 1003 CE)\n Didda’s life and career are chronicled in the sixth taranga \nof the Rajatarangini.\n Married to King Kshemagupta, she was the daughter \nof Simharaja of Lohara, situated around the Pir Panjal \nrange, a crucial trade route between Western Punjab \nand Kashmir. Additionally, she was the granddaughter of \nBhima Shahi, one of the Hindu Shahis of Kabul.\n Initially served as a regent for her son and various \ngrandsons, eventually ascending to the throne as the \nsole ruler.\n Kalhana, the chronicler, disapproved of Didda, portraying \nher as reflecting the perceived defects of womankind. He \ncriticized her moral character, describing her as merciless \nand detailing how she ruthlessly killed her son and three \ngrandsons before taking the throne.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nHow do you see the role of powerful queens, such \nas Yashovati, Sugandha, and Didda in the unique \ncultural feature of Kashmiri society and the broader \nsocietal expectations and opportunities for women in \nleadership positions across medieval India?\n According to Kalhana, Didda was involved in an affair \nwith a courier and herdsman named Tuna, who later \nbecame her trusted confidante.\n Even before her regency, Didda wielded considerable \ninfluence in state affairs, as evidenced by coins bearing \nboth her name and that of Kshemagupta.\n She founded the towns of Diddapura and Kankanapura\nand established a temple named the Diddasvamin.\nAfter both indirect and direct rule, Didda strategically placed \nSangramaraja, her brother’s son, on the throne. This move diverted \nthe royal succession from the Yashaskaras to her own natal family, \nmarking the inception of the Lohara dynasty in Kashmir."
]
