    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: [
  "40 Medieval India \nHINDU SHAHI DYNASTY\nAfter the decline of the Kushana Empire in the 3rd century \nto the early 9th century CE, the Kabul valley (in Eastern \nAfghanistan) and Gandhara (Northern Pakistan and Kashmir) \nwere ruled by the Turkish Shahiya dynasty. The Shahis were \ndivided into two eras: The Buddhist Shahis and the later \nHindu Shahis, with the transition into Hinduism occurring \naround 870 CE. \nIn the second half of the 9th century CE, Kallar, a Brahmana \nminister of King Lagaturman, overthrew the Shahiya king \nand established the foundation of the Hindu Shahi dynasty. \nThe Shahis ruled over predominantly Buddhist and Hindu \npopulations, making them patrons of various faiths, and \nnumerous artefacts and coins from their rule have been \ndiscovered, showcasing their multicultural domain.\nKallar (c. 890–895 CE)\n In the Rajatarangini, Kallar is mentioned as Lalliyashahi and \nserved as the Brahmana minister of the Hindu Shahi Dynasty.\n His control over the Kabul valley was short-lived, and \naround 870 CE, he had to relocate his capital from Kabul \nto Udabhanda (modern-day Und village in Rawalpindi \ndistrict, Pakistan) after being defeated by the Arab, \nSarrarid Yaqui ibn.\nJayapala (c.964–1001 CE)\n Jayapala was celebrated as a hero for his efforts in defending \nhis kingdom against the Turkish rulers of Ghazni.\n As per legends, in the Battle of Peshawar (c.1000 CE), \nKing Jayapala faced challenges from the armies of Sultan \nSabuktigin and later from his son, Sultan Mahmud of Ghazni.\nAnandapala (c.1001 −1011 CE)\n The son of Jayapala, Anandapala was a skilled warrior \nand general who led many battles before ascending to \nthe throne. \n During his reign, the Ghaznavids inflicted significant \nlosses on his kingdom. \n• In the Battle of Chach against Mahmud, around \n30,000 Gakhars fought alongside Anandapala, \nresulting in substantial losses for the Ghaznavids. \n• Despite putting up a strong resistance, Anandapala \nsigned a treaty with the Ghaznavid Empire in c.1010 \nCE and passed away peacefully a year later. \n Some historians compare his brave opposition to \nMahmud of Ghazni with the resistance of King Porus \nagainst Alexander.\nTrilochanpala (c.1010−1022 CE)\n He attempted to expand his kingdom by conquering the \nShivalik Hills, controlled by the Rai of Sharwa.\n His kingdom stretched from the River Indus to the upper \nGanges valley, showcasing his ambitious territorial \nambitions. \n Al-Biruni notes Trilochanpala’s initial commitment to his \nfather’s peace treaty (honorable in honoring his father’s \npeace treaty) with the Ghaznavids, but circumstances led \nto his rebellion against Sultan Mahmud.\n He eventually rebelled against Sultan Mahmud and was \nassassinated in 1022 CE, allegedly instigated by the Rai \nof Sharwa.\n Trilochanpala’s legacy lived on in Punjabi folklore, where \nhe was romanticized as the last Punjabi ruler of Punjab.\nBheempala (c.1022–1026 CE)\n He was referred to as the ‘fearless’ by historian Utbi for \nhis courage and valor.\n He commanded the Battle of Nandana, where he \nseriously wounded the commander of the Ghaznavid \narmy, Muhammad bin Ibrahim.\n Bheempala, the final Shahi Emperor of the illustrious \ndynasty, faced the ultimate downfall when Mahmud \nof Ghazni razed their kingdom at the onset of the 12th \ncentury CE.\n• Their remaining army was eventually exiled to \nNorthern India.\nIn the complex tapestry of the Hindu Shahi dynasty, each \nruler left a distinct mark. From Kallar’s establishment to \nBheempala’s valiant stand, the Shahis navigated cultural \ndiversity, facing challenges from Arab and Ghaznavid \nforces. Despite courageous resistance, Mahmud of Ghazni’s \nconquest marked the dynasty’s end, leading to their exile \nin Northern India. The Shahi legacy lives on, remembered \nin historical accounts and Punjabi folklore, embodying a \nchapter of resilience and territorial ambition in the rich \ntapestry of medieval Indian history.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nDo you think the establishment of the Hindu Shahi \ndynasty by Kallar marked a significant shift in the \npolitical and cultural dynamics of the Kabul valley and \nGandhara? How might this transition have influenced \nthe relationships between different religious \ncommunities within the region?\nTHE CHEDIS OF TRIPURI\nThe Kalachuri dynasty governed the state of Chedi, located \nbetween the Narmada and Godavari rivers. Initially \nsubordinated to the Pratiharas, the Kalachuris of Chedi, \nalso known as the Kalachuris of Tripuri, asserted their \nindependence around the mid-10th century CE. Their rule \nextended over the historical Chedi region, known as Dahala￾mandala, with the capital situated at Tripuri, near modern \nJabalpur, MP. Engaging in battles against the Chandellas of \nJejakabhukti and forming matrimonial alliances with them, \nthe Kalachuris of Tripuri also defended their territory from \nthe Palas and a confederacy comprising the Cholas, Pallavas, \nand the Pandyas."
]
