    id: "m3-pdf-page-42",
    chapter: "03 • Northern India (c. 1000–1200 CE)",
    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 42 — Northern India (c. 1000- 1200 CE) 41 Kokkala I (c.845−885 CE)  Kokkala I, the founder of the Kalachuri dynasty, embarked",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: [
  "Northern India (c. 1000- 1200 CE) 41\nKokkala I (c.845−885 CE)\n Kokkala I, the founder of the Kalachuri dynasty, embarked \non numerous military campaigns, including an invasion \nof Northern Konkan. \n He allied with the Rashtrakuta king, Krishna II, against \nthe Eastern Chalukyas and the Pratiharas. \n After Kokkala I’s death, the kingdom faced fragmentation \nas his eldest son succeeded him on the throne, while \nothers were appointed rulers of different mandalas/\nprovinces. \n• This led to the disintegration of the kingdom, with one \nof his descendants establishing a separate kingdom \nin South Kosala, with its capital at Tummana.\nYuvaraja (c. 915–945 CE)\n Yuvaraja, another notable ruler, achieved success by \ndefeating and driving out the Rashtrakuta forces. \n The renowned poet Rajasekara staged his drama, \nViddhasalabhanjika, to celebrate this victory. \n The Kalachuri inscription credits Yuvaraja with successful \nraids against Kashmir and the Himalayan region.\nLakshmanaraja (around the third quarter \nof the 10th century CE)\n A formidable conqueror, Lakshmanaraja expanded his \nkingdom by defeating Vangala (south Bengal) and the \nSomavamsi king of South Kosala. \n He also invaded Lata in the west, ruled by a Rashtrakuta \nfeudatory, and defeated the king of Gurjara, likely \nMularaja I, founder of the Chalukya Dynasty.\nGangeya-deva (c.1019–40 CE)\n Gangeya-deva, one of the most important rulers of the \nKalachuri dynasty, elevated the Chedis to one of the most \nsignificant political forces in India through his military \ngenius. \n As a token of his great victory, he assumed the titles \nof Trikalingadhipati (the Lord of Trikalinga) and \nVikramaditya. \n Some historians attribute his success to the fact that \nhis kingdom escaped the devastating raids of Sultan \nMahmud. \n Gangeya-deva introduced coins of various sizes, weights, \nand metals during his rule, featuring Lakshmi with four \narms sitting in a cross-legged position on the lotus, with \ninscriptions in Nagari script ‘Shrimad- Gangeyadeva’ on \nthe reverse side.\nLakshmi-Karna (c.1041–73 CE)\n Lakshmi-Karna, also known as Karna, inherited his \nfather’s military prowess, emerging as one of the greatest \ngenerals of his time. \n His success extended not only in the south but also in \nthe north-west, and he carried out victorious campaigns \nalong the Eastern coast, reaching as far as Kanchi. \n His conquests included victories over dynasties in the \nsouth, such as the Pallavas, Kungas, Muralas, Pandyas \n(South), and Someshvara I.\nYashkarna (c.1073–1125 CE)\n Yashkarna’s reign faced a series of invasions, with \nthe Chalukyas of Deccan raiding his kingdom and the \nParamaras plundering his capital, encamping on the \nNarmada. \n Defeats by the Chandellas and Chalukyas gradually made \nthe Kalachuris weak.\nVijayasimha (c.1177−1211 CE)\n The last king of the Kalachuri dynasty, Vijayasimha, \nwitnessed the conquest of nearly the entire Kalachuri \nkingdom by the Chandella ruler Trailokyavarman. \n Baghelkhand and Dahala Mandala were included in the \nChandella conquest.\nTowards the beginning of the 12th century CE, the Kalachuri \nkingdom faced further challenges as the Chedi possessions \nwere lost to rising dynasties like the Ganpatis of Warangal, \nthe Yadavas of Devagiri, and the Vaghela Rajputs.\nTHE SENAS OF BENGAL\nThe Sena Dynasty, a Hindu Dynasty that ruled over Bengal \nfor a little over a century (c. 1070-1230 CE), extended its \nempire across much of the northEastern region of the Indian \nsubcontinent. Before their dominance, Bihar and Bengal were \nunder the influence of the Buddhist Pala dynasty. Identifying \nthemselves as Brahma Kshatriya, in contrast to the Buddhist \nPalas, the Sena rulers were staunch Hindus. Originating \nfrom the southern region of Karnataka, as revealed in the \nDeopara inscription, the Sena Dynasty claimed its lineage. \nThe inscription references Samanta Sen, one of the founders \nin the South, with his successor Hemanta Sen being the first \nto adopt royal titles, seizing power from the Palas around \nc. 1095 CE. Vijay Sena, his successor, played a crucial role in \nestablishing the foundations of the dynasty.\nVijaya Sena (c.1096-1159 CE)\n Vijaya Sena, the founder of the Sena Dynasty, had a \nremarkably long reign, lasting over 60 years. \n His rule brought peace and prosperity to Bengal, making \na lasting impression on its people. \n Taking advantage of the revolt of Samantachakra in \nVarendra, he gradually consolidated his position in \nWestern Bengal and ultimately assumed independence \nduring the reign of Madanapala."
]
