    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: [
  "42 Medieval India \n His conquests included Vanga, where he defeated \nBhojavarman, and he established two capitals, \n• Vikrampura and \n• Vijaypura. \n Vijay Sena was commemorated in the Vijay Prasasti, an \neulogy composed by the renowned poet Sriharsha.\n The Deopara Prashasti, a stone inscription crafted by \nthe court poet Umapati Dhara, and the Barrackpore \ncopper plate both hailed Vijay Sena as Maharajadhiraja, \nemphasizing his royal stature.\nBallal Sena (c.1159−1179 CE)\n Ballal Sena, the renowned Sena ruler, played a crucial role \nin consolidating the kingdom and expanding its territories.\n• His conquests included Mithila in Eastern Bihar, and \nhe ruled over the five provinces of Banga, Barendra, \nRar, Bagri, and Mithila. \n Nabadwip became the capital under his rule. \n Ballal Sena is famously associated with the revival of \northodox Hindu practices in Bengal, particularly the \nestablishment of the reactionary tradition of Kulinism. \n He solidified ties with South India by marrying Ramadevi, \na princess of the Western Chalukya Empire. \n Among his literary contributions are the works \nDanasagara and Adbhutsagara.\nLakshamana Sena (c.1179-1206 CE)\n Lakshmana Sena, a key figure in the Sena dynasty, \nmarked the pinnacle of military glory. Under his rule, the \nSena Empire expanded to encompass Assam, Odisha, \nBihar, and potentially Varanasi. \n Notably, he defeated Jayachandra of the Gahadavalas \ndynasty and erected pillars to commemorate his military \nachievements in various regions. \n Lakshmana Sena became the first Bengali ruler to extend \nsuzerainty beyond Benaras. \n He adopted the titles Ariraja-Madana-Sankara and \nParamvaishnava, indicating his devout Vaishnavism, a \ndeparture from the Shaiva tradition of his predecessors. \n His court flourished with poets like Jayadeva, Dhoyi, and \nUmapati Dhar. Despite his military successes, the later \nyears of his reign witnessed the disintegration of the \nSena kingdom, losing control over South Bengal, Orissa, \nand Kamrupa, and facing invasions from Central Asian \nIslamic forces.\nIn the early 13th century, circa 1203–1204 CE, Muhammad Bin \nBaktiyar Khalji, a general of Muhammad Ghori, launched an \nattack on Nabadwip, leading to the invasion and annexation of \nBengal and Bihar into the Islamic empire. While he successfully \ncaptured north-west Bengal, the Eastern part remained under \nSena control. However, by the mid-13th century, the Deva \ndynasty had toppled the Senas from power.\nTHE GANGAS/CHODAGANGAS \nOF ORISSA\nFig. 3.12: The Gangas/Chodagangas of Orissa\nThe Eastern Ganga dynasty, known as the Chodagangas, left \nan indelible mark on Kalinga’s history, reigning from the 11th \nto the early 15th century. Their dominion extended across \nmodern-day Odisha, parts of West Bengal, Andhra Pradesh, \nand Chhattisgarh, with the capital situated in Kalinganagara, \nnow Srimukhalingam in Andhra Pradesh. \nFounded by King Anantavarman Chodaganga, the dynasty \ntraced its lineage to the Western Ganga Dynasty in southern \nKarnataka and the Chola Dynasty. Hailing from southern \nIndia and connected through matrimonial ties with the Chola \nempire and Eastern Chalukyas, the Eastern Ganga rulers \nbrought the rich tapestry of south Indian culture to Odisha. \nThis cultural infusion is distinctly evident in their currency.\nThe wealth amassed from trade and commerce was primarily \nchanneled into the construction of grand temples, shaping \nthe architectural and artistic landscape of the region.\nThe Kesaris, predecessors to the Gangas in ruling Odisha, had \npreviously erected the Lingaraja temple in Bhubaneshwar. \nHowever, it was under the Chodagangas’ rule that the \ngrandeur of Odishan architecture reached new heights with \nthe construction of the iconic Sun Temple at Konark. This \ntemple, a testament to their cultural and artistic prowess, \nstands as a lasting legacy, symbolizing the zenith of their \nkingdom’s prosperity and cultural refinement."
]
