    id: "m3-pdf-page-45",
    chapter: "03 • Northern India (c. 1000–1200 CE)",
    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 45 — 44 Medieval India The Later Western Chalukyas, reigning from Kalyani (modern Basavakalyan in Karnataka) during the 10th to",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: [
  "44 Medieval India \nThe Later Western Chalukyas, reigning from Kalyani \n(modern Basavakalyan in Karnataka) during the 10th to \n12th centuries CE, played a significant role in the Deccan and \nsouthern India. Before their ascendancy, the Rashtrakuta \nempire of Manyakheta had dominion over much of Deccan \nand central India for over two centuries.\nIn 973 CE, Taila or Tailapa II, a feudatory of the Rashtrakutas \nruling from the Bijapur region, seized an opportunity during \nthe confusion caused by the Paramaras of Malwa invading \nManyakheta. Tailapa II defeated his overlords and established \nManyakheta as his capital. This marked the beginning of the \nLater Western Chalukya dynasty’s rise to power.\nUnder the rule of Someshvara I, the dynasty flourished and \nexpanded into a formidable empire. Notably, the capital was \nmoved to Kalyani, solidifying its influence in the region.\nTailapa-II (c.973−997 CE)\n Founder of the Later Western Chalukyas, he consolidated \nhis realm with the support of the earlier Chalukya family, \nthe Kadambas, and successfully recovered lost territories.\n The Kadambas were rewarded with control over Banavasi \nand Goa.\nSatyasraya (c.997−1008 CE)\n He succeeded Tailapa-II and achieved victory against \nChola king Rajaraja, who had invaded his region.\n Conquered territories of Rajaraja in the process.\n Successors include Dasavarman, Vikramaditya V, and \nJayasimha I (c. 1015−1042 CE).\n Jayasimha I repelled invasions from the north and \nsouthern invasions from Chola King Rajendra.\nSomeshvara-I (c.1042−1068 CE)\n Son of Jayasimha, expelled Rajadhiraja Chola, although \nthe capital had already been plundered by the Cholas.\n Broke the line of succession of Rajendra Chola by killing \nhim.\n Following tradition, Someshvara I expressed a preference \nfor his second son, Vikramaditya, as his successor. \nHowever, Vikramaditya declined the honor in favor of \nthe elder Somesvara II (c. 1068−1076 CE). \n As a general in his brother’s army, Vikramaditya went on \nto conquer the Cholas, Kerala, and Ceylon. Unfortunately, \nSomesvara II’s descent into ill-advised behavior led to the \nloss of his brother’s loyalty. Consequently, Vikramaditya \nassumed independent rule over the southern part of the \nkingdom.\nVikramaditya VI (c.1076−1127 CE)\n Considered the greatest ruler of the dynasty, earned \ntitles like Permadideva and Tribhuvanamalla (meaning \n“lord of three worlds”).\n Received submission from the ruler of Konkan and \nmarched against Vira Rajendra Chola.\n Placed his brother-in-law, Adhi-Rajendra, on the throne \nof Cholas, after the demise of Vira Rajendra.\n Defeated by Eastern Chalukya king Jayasimha, after his \nbrother-in-law was killed, and given the governorship of \nBellary, but reclaimed power in c.1076 CE with the aid of \nthe Hoysalas and ascended the throne as Vikramaditya \nVI. He also introduced the Chalukya−Vikrama era in \nc.1076 CE.\n Under the reign of Vikramaditya VI, the Western \nChalukyas effectively contested with the Cholas, \nestablishing dominance over vast territories across the \nDeccan region. Their rule extended from the Narmada \nRiver in the north to the Kaveri River in the south. \n Vikramaditya VI benefited from the capable assistance of \nseveral queens in the administration of his realm.\n Literary Contributions: In the court of Vikramaditya VI, \nrenowned writers Bilhana and Vijnaneswara flourished. \n• Bilhana, hailing from Kashmir, celebrated Vikramaditya \nVI as the hero in his work “Vikramankadeva Charitra,” \nlikening his rule to Ramarajya. \n• Vijnaneshwara, a distinguished jurist in the \ncourt, authored “Mitakshara,” a commentary on \nYagnavalkya Smriti, praising the king with the words, \n“A King like Vikramarka is neither to be seen nor \nheard of.” \n Vikramaditya VI, a devout follower of Shaivism, left a \nlasting legacy through extensive temple construction, \nincluding notable structures like the Mallikarjuna, \nMahadeva, Kaitabheshvara, and Kalleshvara temples.\nThe subsequent ruler, Somesvara III, showed more \ninclination towards literary pursuits, creating an opening \nfor Vishnuvardhana Hoysala to assert independence. The \nsubsequent rulers lacked significant prowess, leading to a \ngradual decline as Chalukya feudatories sought autonomy. \nDespite this, the Chalukyas managed to reclaim lost territories \nunder the leadership of Somesvara IV, Taila III’s son. His \nauthority was recognized by Singhana, the last Kalachuri \nruler, and he garnered allegiance from the Kadambas of Goa \nand Banavasi, as well as the Pandyas of Uchchangi.\nHowever, challenges arose with the Hoysalas, led by Vira \nBallala I, and the Yadavas of Devagiri launching attacks. These \nconflicts ultimately marked the demise of the later Western \nChalukya dynasty around c. 1190 CE."
]
