    body: [
  "Northern India (c. 1000- 1200 CE) 49\nMimansa\n Mimansa, also known as Purva Mimamsa, signifies the \nart of reasoning and interpretation.\n According to Mimansa, the Vedas contain the eternal \ntruth, and they strongly recommend the performance of \nyagya (Vedic sacrifices) to attain salvation.\n This philosophy relies on the Brahmanas and the \nSamhitas of the Vedas, and it is often argued that the \nBrahmanas aimed to maintain their ritual authority and \npreserve social hierarchy through the propagation of \nMimamsa.\nVedanta\n Vedanta, also known as Uttara Mimamsa, signifies the \nend of the Veda.\n Its basic text, the Brahmaputra of Badarayana, compiled \nin the 2nd century BCE, forms the foundation of Vedanta.\n According to Vedanta, the world is Maya (illusion), \nemphasizing the existence of only one reality—Brahman.\n Salvation is sought through brahmagyan, with Vedanta \nrelying on the Upanishads for its philosophical \nunderpinnings.\n Rooted in Samkhya philosophy, Vedanta posits that the \nself, or atman (soul), is identical to Brahman, leading to \nsalvation through self-knowledge.\n The theory of karma is closely linked to Vedanta \nphilosophy, signifying that one bears the consequences \nof actions from previous births.\n The fundamental principle of Vedanta is that all actions \nshould be guided by the discriminating faculty of the \nintellect.\n There are three sub-branches of Vedanta:\n• Advaita Vedanta (non-dualism) of Shankara:\n Shankara, a prominent figure in the 9th century, \nadvocated Advaita Vedanta.\n In this philosophy, brahman is considered without \nattributes, and knowledge (jnana) is seen as the \nprimary means of salvation.\n Shankara posited the complete identity of atman \nwith brahman, asserting the existence of only one \nreality.\n Maya, the delusive power, causes a false sense of \nplurality and individuality in the atman.\n The only true reality, according to Shankara, is \nbrahman—pure consciousness, eternal, and \nunchanging.\n He distinguishes between conventional reality \nand absolute reality, emphasizing that differences \narise due to ignorance.\n Devotion to God and understanding the oneness \nof atman with brahman are pathways to salvation.\n• Visistadvaita Advaita (Qualified Non-dualism) of \nRamanuja:\n Ramanuja, in the 12th century, combined \nVaishnava bhakti with Upanishadic monism.\n He identified brahman with Vishnu, asserting \nthat brahman possesses attributes (saguna), in \ncontrast to Shankara’s attributeless (nirguna) \nbrahman.\n Salvation, according to Ramanuja, is achieved \nthrough practicing devotion or loving faith.\n He likened the relationship between brahman and \natman to that of a red rose and redness, stating \nthat they are distinct but inseparable.\n• Dvaita (Dualism) of Madhva\n Madhva, in the 13th century, originated the \nDvaita school of Hindu Vedanta philosophy.\n According to Madhva, God is completely distinct \nfrom the individual soul and the world.\n He asserted that the individual soul, despite \nits defects, can attain near perfection through \nworshipping God.\n The relationship between God and the soul \nis likened to that of a master and servant in \nMadhva’s philosophy.\n Madhva’s system, known as tattvavada (the realist \nviewpoint), posits three basic elements of reality: \nGod, souls (jivasi), and insentient matter (jada).\n Vishnu is considered preeminent above all other \ndeities in Madhva’s system based on his unique \ncharacteristics.\nIt’s worth noting that while the initial four classical schools \nare not solely rooted in the Vedas, the final two, Mimansa \nand Vedanta, unmistakably draw their foundations from the \nVedas.\nHETERODOX SCHOOLS \nOF INDIAN PHILOSOPHY\nPhilosophies that reject the authority of the Vedas fall into \nthe category of heterodox schools. One such school is:\nCharvaka/Lokayata\n Founded by Charvaka in the final centuries before the \nCommon Era.\n Barhaspatya Sutras, Written by Lord Brahaspati (Teacher \nof Gods), are the foundational texts of the Nastika \nCharvaka school of materialist philosophy. \n Characterized as a materialistic and atheistic school of \nthought."
]
